    "alert_id": "ALERT_001",
    "title": "TDF contraindicated with eGFR <60",
    "message": "Guidelines state TDF is contraindicated when GFR <60 mL/min. Patient's eGFR is 52 mL/min/1.73m² (CKD-EPI), below the 60 mL/min threshold. TDF has been prescribed for 3 years. The patient has no diabetes or hypertension, and urinalysis shows protein 1+, which may indicate TDF-related renal tubular damage. However, other causes of CKD should be excluded. TDF should be discontinued and replaced with TAF, which guidelines state is preferred over TDF.",
    "issue_type": "medication_safety",
    "severity": "critical",
    "recommended_action": "Discontinue TDF immediately and switch to TAF-based regimen. Investigate non-drug causes of renal impairment. Refer to nephrology if indicated.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 10, "chunk_id": "hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__hiv_guidelines_art.extraction__p10_c1"}
    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "ALERT_002",
    "title": "Rising creatinine trend over 5 years",
    "message": "Guidelines require annual serum creatinine and eGFR monitoring for patients on TDF. Patient's creatinine has progressively risen from 0.9 (2020) to 1.4 mg/dL (2026), with eGFR declining to 52 mL/min/1.73m². This steady worsening over 5 years signals ongoing renal injury even if individual values were previously within range. Protein 1+ on urinalysis further supports renal tubular dysfunction. TDF nephrotoxicity is a likely contributor given the temporal relationship with 3 years of TDF use. Non-drug causes should also be evaluated.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Switch from TDF to TAF. Conduct comprehensive renal assessment including renal ultrasound and urine protein-to-creatinine ratio.",
